    "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent Identity: Replacing a Squared Secant",
    "problem": "$\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}=$",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\,{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{2\\,(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2{tan}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}=1.\n$$",
